From d4a6ba6c8570bc8a3a64a7161862c491cc16acb6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hoangvu12 <hggaming91@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 09:24:41 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] Attachments to another device resume after a dropped link,
 and keep trying while their message waits

A file sent to a chat on another of the person's devices went over the
peer link one 60,000-character chunk at a time, each waiting for the
host's answer before the next. When the link dropped, the next attempt
started again from the file's first byte, and after 15 minutes of
attempts the sender gave up and the host rejected the message
("attachments never arrived"). A link that drops every minute or two
(seen on 2026-11-25 between a laptop and a PC) could not carry a file
that took longer than the gap between drops: retrying sometimes worked,
and sometimes nothing ever did.

- UploadStatus (new): the host says what it holds of an upload, committed
  or which chunks are staged and how long each is. Asking counts as
  hearing from the sender. Hosts without it are sent everything.
- The sender asks first, then sends only the missing chunks, six in
  flight at once instead of one.
- Chunks are written aside and renamed, so a chunk the host reports is
  whole.
- The sender keeps trying for as long as its command waits (pending and
  unexpired; a queued row while it's in the queue, up to a day), not for
  a fixed 15 minutes. The command relay does the same.
- The host waits for a command's bytes until it hasn't heard from their
  sender for 15 minutes, instead of 15 minutes after the send.
- zgx hands the files of a message to a chat on any other device to its
  engine, which delivers them as above, as it did for Cloud chats only.
  It uploaded them to the host inside the send before, relay chunk by
  relay chunk, so a link dropping mid-file failed the whole send ("Not
  delivered"). A chat on this device, or an engine too old to know the
  attachment store, still uploads straight to its host.

Tests: uploads' status_says_what_is_staged_so_a_sender_can_resume,
doc_host's transfer_resume_tests, tests/flaky_link_attachments.rs (a
34-chunk file through a device room that cuts the link every 10 chunks),
and zgx's the_engine_delivers_files_to_any_other_device_once_it_knows_how.
